WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.059 --> 00:00:03.870
Good morning Hank; it’s Wednesday. That’s
not a green-screen, I’m really in Amsterdam.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.680
So Hank I’m here in Amsterdam for two months
finishing my new book. The reason I’m in

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.040
Amsterdam will become obvious when you read
the book. Also the Yeti needs to be in Europe

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.920
because she’s working on the U.F. pavilion
for the Venice Biennale, very fancy. And so

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.919
every body has a reason to be here – say
“Hello” to the tram – except for Henry,

00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:20.099
but, you know, he’s portable.

00:00:20.099 --> 00:00:22.380
By the way just to orient you, we’re going
to the flower market.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.640
Right Hank, so back in the early sixteen-hundreds,
when tulips first came to Holland, they were

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:30.109
prized for the richness of their color. But
there were these multi-coloured ones that

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:33.840
were particularly beloved. So most tulips
look like the tulips you’re used to seeing,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.640
like these. But there were these rare and
fragile and sickly looking bulbs that made

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.050
beautiful tulips, like the Viceroy. More on
him in a second.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.290
Right, so, throughout the first half of the
1630s – don’t worry, this is not going

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:49.780
to hit me – the price of tulips rose pretty
steadily, and by the winter of 1636, Hank,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.970
the Dutch had become total tulip maniacs.
Particularly for those sickly-looking bulbs

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:59.580
that made brilliant flowers, like the aforementioned
Viceroy. For the amount of money a single

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:05.199
Viceroy bulb cost in 1637 you could have bought
all of this. I mean, Hank, forget the rye

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:08.310
and the fat oxen. I think I could live happily
ever after with just a suit of clothes, a

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:12.149
complete bed, and 4000 pounds of butter. That
comment probably didn’t make sense to the

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:16.920
people standing behind me. And then in 1637,
pretty much all at once, the market for tulip

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:22.869
bulbs completely collapsed, falling like 99%
in two months. It was one of the first great

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:26.979
bubbles of capitalism, Hank, but that’s
not what fascinates me, here’s what fascinates

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:27.979
me.

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:31.490
Those fragile-looking bulbs that made brilliant
flowers like the Viceroy, it turns out they

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.009
were sick with a virus called the tulip mosaic
virus. They’ve all long since died out,

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:40.310
and while we can look at paintings, we will
never know quite what it felt like to see

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:45.170
the Viceroy. I first heard of tulip mania
in Atlas by Katrina Vandenberg, this brilliant

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.250
book of poems that has shaped my think a lot
while writing this new book. Here’s how

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:54.770
she put it, "Aren’t you sorry you will never
see a tulip that would make you offer all

00:01:54.770 --> 00:02:00.670
you own for the layered, translucent promise
in its brown paper wrapper?"

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.249
Hank, I’ll see you on Friday.

